 ## Changelog
 
+- 2026-10-20 (8): FIX, tall walls beside a river whose water lies far below (two device screenshots: terraced dirt cliffs of 10..20 blocks on both sides of a low river). Tested in a Linux scratch crate holding `hydro.rs` + `biome.rs` only (rustc 1.75): all 13 tests there pass, incl. the new one; the rest of the crate was not compiled or run, not checked in game.
+    - Cause (`Region::carve`, bank branch): a river only shaped the ground up to `max(5, 2 * width)` blocks from its channel, and the bank was cut only where the terrain stood at most 10 blocks over its slope (`s - target <= 10`). Beside a hill the slope stopped 5 blocks out at `ws + 3`, so the terrain there was either left whole (more than 10 over: a sheer wall from the channel's edge) or cut and left standing as a wall of up to 10 at the end of the band. The water level (grade of the 16-block grid) is often far under the real terrain of a column, so this showed everywhere there was a hill.
+    - Fix (`hydro.rs`, one function and two consts): `reach(w)` = `max(VALLEY 48, 2 w)` is how far a river still shapes the ground (`Network::plan` and `index_of` use it; `Plan::blend` and the flowers keep the old band). The bank rises `BANK_SLOPE` 0.5 a block from the waterline (was 0.75, only to the band) and over the outer half of the reach eases into the terrain's own height (smoothstep), so the cut ends flush with the land: a hill beside a river is a valley side. The 10 block limit is gone. Low ground (the levee) is unchanged.
+    - Test `hydro::tests::a_river_in_high_ground_has_sloped_banks_not_walls` (a plateau 36 blocks over the water: the old code leaves a wall of 36, the new one steps at most 3 between neighbours, the plateau is untouched at the far edge, the river is there). The old tests (`a_lower_river_does_not_overwrite_a_higher_one`, `the_mouth_opens_into_the_sea`, `carved_water_rests_on_ground`) still pass.
+    - ponytail / UNVERIFIED: a river by a tall mountain now cuts a V-valley up to ~48 blocks a side (the price of no cliff; lower `VALLEY` for narrower valleys, `BANK_SLOPE` for steeper ones); a slope steeper than ~2:1 remains where the hill is more than ~35 blocks over the water; each carved column looks at more segments (the index covers `reach`, about 4x the buckets per segment), unmeasured; chunks already saved keep their old walls (only newly generated chunks are carved; LoD tiles made from `generate_far` need `<world>/lod/` cleared or a tile-magic bump to show it); the lod / chunks tests that generate the default world were not run.
 - 2026-10-20 (7): the title screen's backdrop is the SMANTRI panorama (a rotating cube map) instead of dirt (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run, not seen in game; new test `render::panorama::tests::asset_matches_face_and_shader_validates`, +1 assert in `menu::tests`; run `cargo test --lib --release panorama menu` first).
     - New `render/panorama.rs` (`Panorama`): `assets/panorama.rgba` (6 faces of 512 x 512, `tools/gen_panorama.py smantri_panorama.zip`, resized from the pack's 1024) in one 2D-array texture; one full-screen triangle, the fragment shader turns the view ray into (face, uv) with `GuiMainMenu`'s face order (0 +z, 1 +x, 2 -z, 3 -x, 4 -y, 5 +y). FOV 85, yaw -2 degrees a second, pitch 5 +-10 degrees. `render_menu` draws it inside the menu pass before the HUD, only while `Menu::panorama()` (title screen, not loading); the world list, create, delete, options and loading screens keep the dirt.
     - Checked off-device: a numpy copy of the shader's mapping over the real faces (upright, faces join at every seam). Vanilla's extra 90 degree roll is NOT in the matrix: with it this pack's faces came out on their side.
